  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-12b-2010-p13.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "A cosine plus a sine equals \\(2\\) only when each equals \\(1.\\) So \\(\\cos(2A-B)=1\\) and \\(\\sin(A+B)=1,\\) giving \\(2A-B=0^\\circ\\) and \\(A+B=90^\\circ.\\)\n\nSolving, \\(A=30^\\circ\\) and \\(B=60^\\circ,\\) so \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is a \\(30\\text{-}60\\text{-}90\\) right triangle with the right angle at \\(C.\\)\n\nWith hypotenuse \\(AB=4,\\) the side \\(BC\\) opposite the \\(30^\\circ\\) angle is half the hypotenuse, so \\(BC=2.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Both a cosine and a sine are at most \\(1,\\) so each must equal \\(1\\)",
        "Solve \\(2A-B=0^\\circ\\) and \\(A+B=90^\\circ\\) to identify the triangle"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
